        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.084,
            0.883,
            0.186
          ],
          "content": "Discuss in class other properties of sports balls, such as size, colour, texture and how high they bounce and understand why balls are made up of specific materials for specific sports. Observe Fig. 6.4 and group the objects in as many different ways as possible."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.192,
            0.563,
            0.294
          ],
          "content": "You might have grouped these objects according to their shapes or by their colour or materials they are made up of."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.294,
            0.563,
            0.355
          ],
          "content": "We have learnt that materials may be classified on the basis of their properties."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.356,
            0.565,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": "For example, in the kitchen, we usually store things in such a manner where similar utensils are"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.58,
            0.189,
            0.874,
            0.389
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.614,
                0.396,
                0.84,
                0.414
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.4: Grouping of objects"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.437,
            0.883,
            0.52
          ],
          "content": "placed together. Similarly, a grocer usually keeps all types of spices in one corner, pulses and grains in another corner and so on. You may also visit any chemist shop and enquire about how medicines are arranged."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.537,
            0.801,
            0.588
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "6.3 What are the different Properties of Materials?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.599,
            0.764,
            0.62
          ],
          "content": "Let us learn some more properties of materials."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.637,
            0.859,
            0.661
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "6.3.1 Observe and identify appearance of materials"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.671,
            0.883,
            0.791
          ],
          "content": "Materials often look different from each other. Freshly cut wood, which is unpolished, has a distinct appearance, quite different from that of iron. Similarly, iron looks different from copper or aluminium. However, there might be some similarities among iron, copper and aluminium that make them different from wood."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.79,
            0.885,
            0.892
          ],
          "content": "Let us do a sorting challenge! Collect small pieces of paper, cardboard, wood, chalk, copper wire, aluminium foil and any article made up of brass, bronze, steel, etc. Take a look at the pieces you have collected. Do any of these materials shine when light falls on them? Observe their texture (whether"
        },
